import Foundation

// MARK: - Community Content Expansion (Entries 7–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let communityContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Hebrews 10:24-25",
            scriptureText: "And let us consider how we may spur one another on toward love and good deeds, not giving up meeting together, as some are in the habit of doing, but encouraging one another — and all the more as you see the Day approaching.",
            devotionalTitle: "Don't Go It Alone",
            devotionalText: "Faith was never meant to be a solo project. The writer of Hebrews doesn't just suggest community — they urge it. We need people who spur us on, who notice when we drift, who celebrate our growth. Giving up on gathering is a habit that creeps in slowly. Today, fight for community. Show up even when it's inconvenient. The people of God need you, and you need them.",
            prayerText: "Lord, forgive me for the times I've tried to walk this journey alone. Give me the courage to show up — to be known, to be challenged, to be encouraged. Help me spur others toward love and good deeds. Protect me from the habit of isolation. I need Your people, and they need me. Lead me to community that sharpens my faith. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What has kept you from deeper community with other believers?",
            actionSteps: ["Reach out to one person and schedule a time to connect this week", "Write down the names of three people who encourage your faith and thank God for them"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Acts 2:42-47",
            scriptureText: "They devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and to fellowship, to the breaking of bread and to prayer. Everyone was filled with awe at the many wonders and signs performed by the apostles.",
            devotionalTitle: "The First Church",
            devotionalText: "The early church didn't have programs or buildings. They had devotion — to teaching, fellowship, meals, and prayer. They shared everything and met daily. The result? Awe. People were drawn in because they saw something real. Community isn't about perfection; it's about devotion. When believers commit to showing up for each other, God shows up too.",
            prayerText: "Father, I want the kind of community the early church had — real, devoted, generous. Help me prioritize fellowship the way they did. Not as an afterthought, but as essential to my faith. Give me a hunger for teaching, for breaking bread together, and for praying with others. Let our community be so authentic that it fills people with awe. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Which of the four devotions — teaching, fellowship, breaking bread, prayer — is most missing from your life?",
            actionSteps: ["Invite someone to share a meal with you this week", "Commit to one consistent gathering with other believers"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Galatians 6:2",
            scriptureText: "Carry each other's burdens, and in this way you will fulfill the law of Christ.",
            devotionalTitle: "Burden Bearers",
            devotionalText: "The law of Christ is love — and love shows up with strong shoulders. Carrying someone's burden doesn't mean fixing their problem. It means sitting with them in it, praying through it, and reminding them they're not alone. But here's the harder part: letting someone carry yours. Vulnerability is an act of faith. Today, be willing to both give and receive help.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, You carried the ultimate burden for me. Help me carry burdens for others — not out of obligation, but out of love. And give me the humility to let others help carry mine. Break my pride. Soften my independence. Let me fulfill Your law of love by being present for someone who is struggling. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a burden you've been carrying alone that you need to share with someone?",
            actionSteps: ["Ask someone 'How are you really doing?' and listen without trying to fix anything", "Share one struggle with a trusted friend and ask them to pray for you"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ecclesiastes 4:9-10",
            scriptureText: "Two are better than one, because they have a good return for their labor: If either of them falls down, one can help the other up. But pity anyone who falls and has no one to help them up.",
            devotionalTitle: "Two Are Better",
            devotionalText: "Solomon, the wisest man who ever lived, understood something fundamental: we need each other. Two are better than one — not because one person isn't enough, but because God designed us for partnership. When you fall — and you will — you need someone to help you up. And someone needs you to do the same for them. Isolation isn't independence; it's vulnerability.",
            prayerText: "Lord, thank You for designing me for community. I confess the times I've chosen isolation over connection. Help me build relationships where I can both give and receive support. When I fall, send someone to help me up. When others fall, give me the awareness and courage to be there. Two are better than one, and You are the cord that holds us together. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Who would help you up if you fell today — and do they know you'd do the same for them?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify your 'person' — the one you'd call in a crisis — and tell them what they mean to you", "Check in on someone you haven't heard from in a while"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 12:10",
            scriptureText: "Be devoted to one another in love. Honor one another above yourselves.",
            devotionalTitle: "Honor Above Self",
            devotionalText: "Devotion and honor — two words that reshape every relationship. To be devoted means to show up consistently, not just conveniently. To honor someone above yourself means putting their needs, feelings, and dignity ahead of your own comfort. This isn't weakness — it's Christlikeness. In a world obsessed with self-promotion, honoring others is a radical act of faith.",
            prayerText: "Lord, teach me devotion. Not the kind that's convenient, but the kind that's consistent. Help me honor others above myself — not because they've earned it, but because You've modeled it. Let me be the first to serve, the first to listen, the first to encourage. Transform my relationships through genuine, selfless love. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Who in your life could use more honor and devotion from you?",
            actionSteps: ["Do something kind for someone today without them knowing", "Write an encouraging note to someone and deliver it this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Thessalonians 5:11",
            scriptureText: "Therefore encourage one another and build each other up, just as in fact you are doing.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Ministry of Encouragement",
            devotionalText: "Encouragement isn't flattery — it's spiritual construction. To encourage means to put courage into someone. To build up means to strengthen the foundation of their faith. You have no idea how much a single word of encouragement can change someone's day — or their life. Paul affirmed the Thessalonians for already doing this. Today, keep building.",
            prayerText: "Lord, make me an encourager. Give me eyes to see who needs building up today. Put the right words in my mouth and the right timing in my heart. Help me notice the discouraged, the overlooked, the weary. Use my words as bricks that build up rather than tear down. Let encouragement flow from me like a river. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Whose faith could you strengthen today with a word of encouragement?",
            actionSteps: ["Send three encouraging texts to three different people today", "Look someone in the eye today and tell them something specific you appreciate about them"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Colossians 3:13-14",
            scriptureText: "Bear with each other and forgive one another if any of you has a grievance against someone. Forgive as the Lord forgave you. And over all these virtues put on love, which binds them all together in perfect unity.",
            devotionalTitle: "Forgiveness Binds Us Together",
            devotionalText: "Community without forgiveness is impossible. People will disappoint you, hurt you, and let you down. Paul's instruction is clear: bear with each other and forgive — as the Lord forgave you. That's a high standard. But love is the binding agent that holds everything together. Without forgiveness, community fractures. With it, unity becomes possible even among imperfect people.",
            prayerText: "Lord, help me forgive as You have forgiven me — fully, freely, repeatedly. Where I'm holding grudges, soften my heart. Where I've been hurt, bring healing. Give me the grace to bear with difficult people and the humility to ask forgiveness when I'm the difficult one. Bind us together in love and perfect unity. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone you need to forgive in order to experience deeper community?",
            actionSteps: ["Pray for someone who has hurt you and ask God to bless them", "If there's an unresolved conflict, take one step toward reconciliation today"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 27:17",
            scriptureText: "As iron sharpens iron, so one person sharpens another.",
            devotionalTitle: "Sharpening Each Other",
            devotionalText: "Iron doesn't sharpen iron gently. There's friction, heat, and sparks. Spiritual growth requires relationships that challenge you — friends who love you enough to ask hard questions, call out blind spots, and push you toward Christlikeness. Comfortable friendships are nice, but sharpening friendships are transformative. Seek people who make you better, not just people who make you comfortable.",
            prayerText: "Lord, bring iron-sharpening relationships into my life. I don't just want comfort — I want growth. Give me friends who love me enough to speak truth, and give me the humility to receive it. Help me be that kind of friend to others too — honest, loving, and committed to their growth. Sharpen me, Lord, through Your people. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you have someone in your life who challenges you spiritually? If not, who could fill that role?",
            actionSteps: ["Ask a trusted friend: 'Is there anything you see in me that I should work on?'", "Identify one person whose faith inspires you and spend time with them this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 John 1:7",
            scriptureText: "But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus, his Son, purifies us from all sin.",
            devotionalTitle: "Walking in the Light Together",
            devotionalText: "Fellowship isn't just hanging out — it's walking in the light together. That means transparency, honesty, and vulnerability. When we hide in the dark — concealing our struggles, pretending we're fine — true fellowship is impossible. But when we step into the light, something beautiful happens: genuine connection and the purifying work of Jesus. Authenticity is the doorway to real community.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I want to walk in the light. Give me courage to be honest — with You and with others. Remove the masks I hide behind. I want real fellowship, not surface-level friendships. Help me be transparent about my struggles and gracious with others who are transparent with me. In the light, there is freedom. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What would it look like to be more transparent with the people closest to you?",
            actionSteps: ["Share something honest about your spiritual journey with a trusted friend", "Practice vulnerability by admitting one area where you need prayer"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 15:7",
            scriptureText: "Accept one another, then, just as Christ accepted you, in order to bring praise to God.",
            devotionalTitle: "Acceptance Like Christ's",
            devotionalText: "Christ accepted you before you had it all together — while you were still a mess, still figuring things out, still falling short. That's the standard for how we accept others. Not when they meet our expectations, but as they are. Acceptance doesn't mean agreement on everything. It means choosing to love and include people the way Jesus loves and includes you.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, You accepted me at my worst. Help me extend that same acceptance to others. Where I'm judgmental, make me gracious. Where I'm exclusive, make me welcoming. Let my community reflect Your radical love — a place where imperfect people are accepted and transformed by Your grace. May our unity bring praise to Your name. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone you've been slow to accept that Christ has already welcomed?",
            actionSteps: ["Welcome someone who seems like an outsider in your community", "Reflect on how Christ accepted you and write a prayer of gratitude"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ephesians 4:2-3",
            scriptureText: "Be completely humble and gentle; be patient, bearing with one another in love. Make every effort to keep the unity of the Spirit through the bond of peace.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Effort Unity Requires",
            devotionalText: "Paul says 'make every effort' — unity doesn't happen passively. It requires humility when you'd rather be right, gentleness when you'd rather react, patience when you'd rather walk away. The bond of peace isn't automatic; it's maintained through intentional, Spirit-empowered love. Every relationship worth having requires effort. Today, choose to invest in unity.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me humility, gentleness, and patience — the building blocks of unity. Help me make every effort to maintain peace in my relationships. Where there's tension, make me a peacemaker. Where there's division, make me a bridge. I can't do this in my own strength — I need Your Spirit. Bind us together in love. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Where do you need to make more effort to maintain unity in a relationship?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one relationship where you need more patience and ask God for it", "Reach out to someone you've had tension with and take a step toward peace"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Peter 4:8-10",
            scriptureText: "Above all, love each other deeply, because love covers over a multitude of sins. Offer hospitality to one another without grumbling. Each of you should use whatever gift you have received to serve others.",
            devotionalTitle: "Deep Love, Open Doors",
            devotionalText: "Peter gives three commands that build community: love deeply, offer hospitality, and serve with your gifts. Deep love covers offenses rather than keeping score. Hospitality opens your home and heart without grumbling. Using your gifts serves others rather than elevating yourself. Together, these three practices create the kind of community people are desperate to find.",
            prayerText: "Lord, help me love deeply — not surface-level, but sacrificially. Open my home and heart to others without complaint. Show me the gifts You've given me and help me use them to serve. I want to be part of a community marked by deep love, radical hospitality, and faithful service. Make me that kind of person. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Which of the three — deep love, hospitality, or serving with your gifts — do you need to grow in most?",
            actionSteps: ["Invite someone into your home this week for a meal or coffee", "Identify one gift God has given you and find a way to use it for someone else"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 13:34-35",
            scriptureText: "A new command I give you: Love one another. As I have loved you, so you must love one another. By this everyone will know that you are my disciples, if you love one another.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Mark of a Disciple",
            devotionalText: "Jesus didn't say the world would know His followers by their theology, their worship style, or their social media posts. He said they'd know by love. Love is the unmistakable mark of a disciple. Not sentimental love, but sacrificial love — the kind Jesus modeled by washing feet and dying on a cross. When the church loves well, the world takes notice.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, let love be the mark of my life. Not just my words, but my actions. Help me love as You loved — sacrificially, unconditionally, without expecting anything in return. Let the way I treat others point people to You. Make our community so marked by love that the world can't help but notice something is different. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "If someone observed your life for a week, would love be the most obvious thing about you?",
            actionSteps: ["Do one sacrificial act of love for someone today that costs you time or comfort", "Ask God to show you one person who needs to experience His love through you"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Philippians 2:3-4",
            scriptureText: "Do nothing out of selfish ambition or vain conceit. Rather, in humility value others above yourselves, not looking to your own interests but each of you to the interests of the others.",
            devotionalTitle: "Others First",
            devotionalText: "This verse flips the world's script. Culture says look out for number one. Paul says look out for everyone else. Valuing others above yourself isn't about having low self-esteem — it's about having a high view of the people God has placed in your life. When you make someone else's interests your priority, you reflect the heart of Jesus, who made your interests His priority on the cross.",
            prayerText: "Lord, root out selfish ambition and vain conceit from my heart. Give me genuine humility — the kind that values others and considers their needs before my own. Help me see people the way You see them: valuable, worthy, and loved. Transform my self-centered tendencies into Christ-centered service. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In your closest relationships, whose interests do you tend to prioritize — yours or theirs?",
            actionSteps: ["Ask someone 'What do you need today?' and follow through on it", "Before making a decision this week, consider how it affects the people around you"]
        ),
    ]
}
